course: dictionary
lesson: 12675
title: Telling things apart
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To distinguish means to see or understand the difference between two or more things. If you can tell a cat from a rabbit, you can distinguish them. «Can you distinguish a real coin from a fake one?» asks whether you can spot the difference.
explain: You can use distinguish between two things, or distinguish A from B. «She can distinguish between a fact and an opinion.» «Can you distinguish the twins from each other?» Both mean the same: you notice what makes them different.
explain: Distinguish can also mean to see or hear something clearly. «In the fog, I could just distinguish a tall shape by the gate.» It can also mean to be the thing that makes something different. «Its long neck distinguishes the giraffe.» Its long neck is what sets it apart.
explain: A quick check: swap in «tell apart» or «see the difference». If the sentence still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
fact: To distinguish means to see or understand the difference between things.
fact: «Distinguish between» is used when you compare two or more things, such as «distinguish between living and non-living things».
fact: «Distinguishing features» are the marks that make one thing different from another, such as the stripes on a zebra.
fact: «Distinguish yourself» means to do something so well that people notice and respect you.
fact: Scientists, readers and people staying safe all need to distinguish: fact from opinion, and safe from unsafe.
exercise: «Can you distinguish a real coin from a fake one?» What does distinguish mean here? | Count them quickly | Make them look the same | Throw them away | *Tell the difference between them
exercise: «Its long neck distinguishes the giraffe.» What does this sentence mean? | The giraffe is sad about its neck | *The long neck makes a giraffe different from other animals | The neck is the biggest part of a giraffe | The giraffe has a long neck and a short tail
exercise: In a safety lesson, what must you learn to distinguish? | Big words from small words | The sound of rain from the sound of a bell | *Real messages from scams | Your favourite colour from your friend's
test: «She tried to distinguish between a fact and an opinion in the article.» What is she trying to do? | *Tell which sentence is true information and which is someone's view | Read the article as fast as she can | Find the longest word in the article | Copy the article onto her page
test: «Even in the dark, I could just distinguish a shape near the tree.» What does distinguish mean here? | Climb up the tree | Hear a loud noise | Forget where the tree is | *See or notice something clearly
exam: «Sam wanted to distinguish himself in the school race.» What does distinguish himself mean? | Copy the winner's shoes | Stay at home and rest | *Do something so well that people notice and respect him | Hide from the other runners
